From 6efb83a0285d81ec72cd6c73aab7f4899ea1c409 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Eden (Doris) build" <doris@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 02:03:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Eden (Doris): textures created from GPU drawings stay
 GPU-drawn; same-layout textures sharing memory kept in step; cleanup copies
 back the newest drawing; report names textures kept apart and why, and chip
 temperatures

- JoinImages copies what the GPU drew into an alias (only GPU-drawn aliases are copied) into
  a new image, but left the new image marked as a plain upload. Its next write by the game's
  code anywhere on its pages then uploaded all of it again from guest memory (which never got
  the drawing), and SynchronizeAliases never brought the drawing back (the alias isn't newer),
  until the game drew it again. Blits and DMA copies also read it from guest memory. The new
  image is now marked GPU-drawn, as SynchronizeAliases does.
- Images sharing memory that Eden can't join ("bad overlap") never exchanged contents. Where
  both lay the shared memory out the same way (bytes per texel, pitch in GOBs, GOBs per block,
  a whole number of block rows apart), the newer GPU drawing is now copied over when the other
  is used (checked on a PC against Eden's own unswizzling).
- The texture cleanup brings a texture up to date from its aliases and same-layout overlaps
  before copying it back, so drawings in single-level ones (removed without a copy back of
  their own) are not lost.
- Report: each pair of textures kept apart, with why each can't be part of the other (logged
  in full the first time), textures created from GPU drawings, same-layout updates, uploads
  next to GPU drawings, format names, and the GPU and hottest CPU/GPU sensor temperatures.

